artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider cosmological models that extend the Standard Model of particle physics (BSM cosmology), in which new physical phenomena (e.g., new particles, fields, primordial black holes, or modified early-universe dynamics) leave imprints on a variety of observational channels. Suppose that one or more of the following experimental hints are independently confirmed as genuine signals of beyond-the-Standard-Model physics:

 (a) an annual-modulation signal of the type reported in direct dark-matter detection with NaI(Tl) targets (DAMA-type experiments);
 (b) gravitational-wave events observed by ground-based interferometers (LIGO–Virgo–KAGRA) whose source population or waveforms cannot be accounted for by Standard Model astrophysics;
 (c) a stochastic gravitational-wave background detected by pulsar timing arrays in the nanohertz band;
 (d) populations of unexpectedly massive or unexpectedly early galaxies at high redshift observed by infrared space telescopes (JWST-type observations);
 (e) a confirmed antihelium component in cosmic rays of the type searched for by space-based magnetic spectrometers (AMS-02-type experiments).

 Develop a systematic methodology for reanalysing existing observational constraints on cosmological parameters and on BSM model parameters under the assumption that any subset of (a)–(e) is confirmed. Specifically:

 1. Formulate quantitatively how each confirmed signal modifies the prior probability distributions and likelihood functions used to derive cosmological bounds (e.g., on dark matter properties, primordial power spectrum, expansion history, baryogenesis scenarios, primordial black hole abundance).
 2. Determine how the “edges” (i.e., boundary regions) of current exclusion contours and confidence regions should be reinterpreted when a previously ignored BSM contribution is included as a genuine effect rather than as a systematic to be marginalized away.
 3. Identify which combinations of (a)–(e), if simultaneously confirmed, lead to mutually consistent BSM cosmological scenarios, and characterize the resulting allowed regions of parameter space.

 Provide a coherent reanalysis framework, or otherwise establish obstructions to such a framework.
